plain text directly inside the `<li>`.\n\nTo select this text using CSS, there are several approaches:\n\n1. **Select the `<li>` element and target its text content directly:**\n   ```css\n   li {\n     /* styles for the li element */\n   }\n   ```\n\n2. **Use the ::before or ::after pseudo-elements if you want to add content before or after the existing text:**\n   ```css\n   li::before {\n     content: \"Custom prefix: \";\n   }\n   ```\n\n3. **Use attribute selectors if you can add a specific attribute to the text:**\n   ```html\n   <li data-label=\"Second level with different code:\">\n   ```\n   Then select it with:\n   ```css\n   li[data-label=\"Second level with different code:\"] {\n     /* styles */\n   }\n   ```\n\n4. **If the text is consistent and you want to target all similar elements:**\n   ```css\n   li::first-line {\n     /* styles for the first line of text in li */\n   }\n   ```\n\n5. **Use JavaScript to wrap the text in a span and then select it:**\n   ```javascript\n   // Wrap the text in a span\n   document.querySelector('li').innerHTML = '<span class=\"label-text\">Second level with different code:</span>' + document.querySelector('li').innerHTML.substring(document.querySelector('li').textContent.indexOf('Second level with different code:') + 'Second level with different code:'.length);\n   ```\n   Then select it with:\n   ```css\n   .label-text {\n     /* styles */\n   }\n   ```\n\nI'll explore additional CSS techniques to select this text. The ::first-letter pseudo-element could target the first character, and the :first-child selector might help if the text is the first element in the parent. Combining pseudo-elements with specific selectors could provide more precise text targeting. The key is finding a reliable method that works across different browsers and maintains the existing text structure.",
