      "text": "\nHmm, the user is asking about a specific CSS layout issue where flex containers without explicit gaps are showing unexpected spacing. Interesting follow-up to my previous explanation about layout shifts! \n\nFirst, let me unpack their scenario: They have multiple flex containers with flex-col and no gap class, yet some show 1-2mm spacing. This is actually a very common CSS gotcha. \n\nThe user seems to be a developer troubleshooting real-world styling. Their tone suggests frustration (\"anehnya ada saja\") - they've probably wasted time chasing phantom margins. The Indonesian phrasing indicates they might be working with a team or framework where someone added default styles.\n\nKey things to consider:\n- The \"gap\" isn't really a gap - it's browser default behavior\n- Multiple containers mean different contexts (maybe some have margins?)\n- Flex items might be inheriting margins from elsewhere\n- Could be framework-specific defaults like Tailwind's space-y\n\nI should emphasize this isn't a layout shift (which is different) but a CSS inheritance issue. The solution is systematic: check margins, frameworks, and browser defaults. \n\nThe user will appreciate concrete debugging steps - they're clearly hands-on. Should mention common culprits like Tailwind's spacing utilities since many use that. Also warn about margin collapsing since they mentioned \"parent container\".\n\nMost importantly: reassure them this is fixable! Many developers get stuck on this. The emoji in my response should match their troubleshooting mood - maybe 🔍 for investigation.",
